    per-browser identity. Costs: absent on first login, so the parameter is optional and often undefined. Moderately
    cheap to change.
  - [1mNot a parameter at all[0m — the server returns the userId in the login response and you log it there. Buys: the
    actually-authoritative ID, since identity is what login [3mestablishes[0m. Costs: doesn't match your literal request, and
    the API call is currently a stub, so nothing returns a real ID yet.

  [1mMy recommendation: the fourth.[0m A login function's job is to determine who the user is; an ID passed [3min[0m by the caller
  can't be trusted as the answer to "who logged in," and here the caller has nothing to pass. That said, the stub
  returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m with no network call, so this option is design-only until the real POST
  exists — which is why the correlation ID is the best option if you want something working today.

